224853515625, 0.0253448486328125, 0.04364013671875, -0.011077880859375, -0.0177459716796875, -0.00841522216796875, 0.029022216796875, -0.0009093284606933594, -0.01244354248046875, 0.044525146484375, -0.04425048828125, 0.0183868408203125, 0.025909423828125, 0.012664794921875, 0.034820556640625, 0.0131988525390625, 0.036865234375, -0.006439208984375, 0.040740966796875, 0.0042877197265625, 0.0277557373046875, -0.019134521484375, 0.0118865966796875, 0.00041937828063964844, -0.01267242431640625, 0.0168914794921875, -0.01007080078125, -0.0013408660888671875, -0.001354217529296875, 0.05731201171875, -0.023468017578125, -0.0184326171875, -0.0263671875, -0.001087188720703125, -0.01425933837890625, -0.01476287841796875, 0.033447265625, 0.025146484375, 0.0103302001953125, -0.025970458984375, -0.00616455078125, 0.001316070556640625, -0.0024089813232421875, -0.0265350341796875, 0.0284881591796875, -0.032012939453125, -0.0157928466796875, -0.0013475418090820312, 0.0004897117614746094, 0.02520751953125, -0.0013217926025390625, -0.00878143310546875, -0.00830078125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Large-scale image generation has advanced through improvements in data scale, quality, rebalancing, and recaptioning. However, most existing pipelines optimize datasets for individual tasks in isolation, limiting the ability to leverage shared knowledge across related generative tasks. In this work, we introduce a **capability-driven data infrastructure**. Here, we define a *capability* as a modular, composable generative function\u2014such as text-to-image grounding, image editing, or associating images with structured knowledge\u2014that can be learned and evaluated independently or in combination. This is distinct from a *task* (a specific objective or benchmark, e.g., generating an image from a caption) and a *skill* (the underlying proficiency required to perform a capability). Capabilities may subsume multiple tasks and require multiple skills.\n\nThis framing enables systematic organization and integration of supervision for multiple generative capabilities, supporting curriculum-based training and interoperability across tasks. Unlike prior work, which typically focuses on single-task curation or ad hoc multi-task extensions (e.g., Ramesh et al., 2022; Saharia et al., 2022; Chen et al., 2023), our framework explicitly models dependencies between capabilities (e.g., grounding as a prerequisite for editing), integrates data engines with shared annotation layers, and schedules data exposure according to a principled curriculum. Table 1 (see Appendix) summarizes key differences: prior pipelines either lack explicit modeling of task dependencies or rely on static multi-task datasets, whereas our approach unifies data curation, annotation, and curriculum scheduling across capabilities.\n\n---\n\n**Motivation and Conceptual Clarity**\n\nThe motivation for this approach is twofold:\n\n1. **Interdependence of Capabilities:** Many generative capabilities are not independent. For example, effective image editing requires robust grounding in text-image alignment.\n2. **Benefits of Curriculum-Based Training:** Curriculum learning, where foundational capabilities are learned before more complex ones, has been shown to improve generalization and sample efficiency (Bengio et al., 2009). Our ablation results (see Table 2) confirm that models trained first on text-image grounding and then on editing outperform those trained in reverse order by 3\u20135% in FID and 4% in editing accuracy.\n\n---\n\n**Capability-Driven Data Infrastructure**\n\nOur infrastructure consists of three interoperable data engines, each responsible for a distinct supervision type, and a curriculum scheduler that sequences data presentation.\n\n**1. Text-Image Grounding Engine**\n\n- **Data Collection:** Crawls public web sources and open repositories for image-caption pairs.\n- **Relevance Scoring:** Uses CLIP ViT-L/14 (OpenAI, 2021) to compute cosine similarity between image and caption embeddings. Pairs with similarity above 0.28 are retained. This threshold was selected based on a pilot study: at 0.28, 95% of pairs were rated relevant by human annotators, while lower thresholds increased false positives.\n- **Toxicity Filtering:** Language model toxicity checks are performed using OpenAI GPT-3.5-turbo, with a toxicity threshold of 0.2 (Perspective API scale). This value was chosen to balance filtering of harmful content with retention of diverse data, as determined by a preliminary annotation study.\n- **Human Annotation:** A random 5% sample is selected for review, stratified by source and content type. Annotators (n=120; 55% female, 45% male; ages 21\u201354; 40% Asian, 30% White, 15% Black, 10% Hispanic, 5% Other) are trained on a 100-example calibration set and required to achieve Fleiss\u2019 kappa >0.8 before contributing.\n- **Annotation Guidelines:** Developed iteratively with annotator feedback and pilot studies, guidelines specify caption relevance, factuality, and sensitivity. Edge cases (e.g., ambiguous object references) are resolved by majority vote, with escalation to a senior annotator if needed.\n- **Error Tracking:** In a 1,000-sample audit, the false positive rate for relevance was 2.1%. Inter-annotator disagreement occurred in 7% of cases, mainly due to subjective judgments on caption specificity.\n- **Caption Standardization:** Caption experts (BLIP-2 models fine-tuned on 1M human-annotated captions) standardize and enrich captions using a fixed prompt template.\n- **Output:** 440M high-quality image-caption pairs.\n\n*Demographic Diversity and Bias Mitigation:* Annotator diversity is prioritized to reduce cultural and linguistic bias in relevance and sensitivity judgments. Regular bias audits are conducted, and annotation guidelines are updated to address observed disparities. Disagreements are analyzed for demographic patterns, and corrective training is provided as needed.\n\n**2. Inter-Image Transformation Engine**\n\n- **Data Sources:** Assembles editing pairs via synthetic edits (Adobe Photoshop batch scripts for color/style changes; Segment Anything Model for object edits) and real-world edits from HIVE and MIT-Adobe FiveK datasets.\n- **Instruction Generation:** Each pair includes the original image, the edited image, and a standardized transformation instruction generated by BLIP-2 caption experts.\n- **Consistency Checks:** Requires a minimum 0.85 cosine similarity between the transformation instruction embedding and the actual edit description (using Sentence-BERT). This threshold was set based on a validation set where 0.85 corresponded to 92% agreement between automated and human-labeled instructions.\n- **Human Validation:** Annotators validate a stratified 5% sample, with inter-annotator agreement >0.78. Disagreements are resolved as in the grounding engine.\n- **Error Tracking:** In a 500-sample audit, annotation error rate for edit correctness was 3.5%, with most disagreements arising from subtle color or style changes.\n- **Output:** 120M editing pairs.\n\n**3. Image-Knowledge Association Engine**\n\n- **Entity Linking:** Links images to structured knowledge entities using BLIP-2 for entity recognition and Wikidata mapping with a minimum confidence of 0.7. This threshold was chosen based on a pilot where higher values reduced recall without improving precision.\n- **Manual Validation:** Performed on a stratified 10% sample. Annotators are trained on a 50-example gold standard and must achieve Fleiss\u2019 kappa >0.8.\n- **Ambiguity Handling:** In cases with multiple plausible entities (e.g., an image of a \"Jaguar\" animal vs. car), annotators document rationale for their choices.\n- **Error Tracking:** In a 300-sample audit, the error rate for entity linking was 4.2%, mostly due to visually ambiguous content.\n- **Output:** 27M image-entity pairs.\n\n---\n\n**Data Integration and Interoperability**\n\nOutputs from the three engines are merged via a shared annotation schema (JSON-LD). Integration challenges include:\n\n- **Conflicting Captions:** For example, two captions for the same image\u2014one generic (\"a person outdoors\") and one specific (\"a woman jogging in a park\"). Resolution: Three independent caption experts and two human annotators review both captions. If consensus is not reached, a senior annotator makes the final decision. In 1,000-sample audits, 4% of records required manual review, often due to such conflicts.\n- **Inconsistent Entity Mappings:** For instance, an image linked to both \"Jaguar (animal)\" and \"Jaguar (car).\" Annotators review context and visual cues, documenting rationale. If ambiguity persists, both entities are retained with uncertainty flags.\n- **Limitations:** Some conflicts remain unresolved due to inherent ambiguity or lack of consensus. These are flagged in the dataset, and users are cautioned about potential inconsistencies.\n\nRedundancies are detected by hashing CLIP image embeddings and deduplicating at the dataset level.\n\n---\n\n**Curriculum Learning Strategy**\n\nThe curriculum scheduler organizes data presentation in three stages:\n\n1. **Text-to-Image (T2I) Grounding**\n2. **Editing**\n3. **Knowledge Association**\n\nStage transitions are triggered when validation loss plateaus for 3 consecutive epochs (<0.1% change) and task-specific thresholds are met (e.g., FID < 10 for T2I, edit accuracy >85% before introducing knowledge data). These thresholds were selected based on ablation studies (see Table 2): models trained with T2I first, then editing, then knowledge, achieved the best performance (FID 8.2, edit accuracy 89%), outperforming all other orderings by 3\u20135% FID and 4% editing accuracy (p < 0.01). The rationale is that foundational grounding skills facilitate more complex transformations and associations. Empirically, reverse or mixed orders resulted in degraded performance.\n\n---\n\n**Evaluation Methodology**\n\n- **Model Training:** Multimodal diffusion models are trained from scratch at 3B and 6B parameters using the curated datasets. Training is conducted on 512 NVIDIA A100 GPUs for 14 days (3B) and 28 days (6B), with mixed-precision optimization and fixed random seeds.\n- **Dataset Splits:** 90% train, 5% validation, 5% test, stratified by source, content, and demographic attributes.\n- **Baselines:** Stable Diffusion v2.1, Imagen Editor, and a BLIP-2-based T2I model, each fine-tuned on our training split for 5 epochs with identical augmentation and optimizer settings.\n\n**Evaluation Metrics:**\n\n- **Automatic Metrics:** FID (Fr\u00e9chet Inception Distance), CLIPScore (semantic alignment), edit accuracy (percentage of edits matching instructions), entity linking accuracy, and diversity score (intra-set image diversity).\n- **Human Ratings:** Aggregated via majority vote across three raters per sample. If disagreement persists, a fourth rater is consulted. Inter-rater reliability is monitored (Fleiss\u2019 kappa >0.75 required)."}}